22.5 Recognising the new key
Look for accidentals that keep coming back and add them to the key signature:

a new flat (or a natural cancelling a sharp) is usually the new key’s scale degree 4;
a new sharp (or a natural cancelling a flat) is often its leading tone (scale degree 7): with several, take the one furthest along the order of sharps;
accidentals that are cancelled again soon are only tonicizations or chromatic non-chord tones.

In the Moonlight: bar 10. The G♯s become G♮ and stay that way: E major has become E minor. Then A♯ appears and keeps coming back, the leading tone of B minor (bars 12–14).
